00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.700
There are no ghosts in the microcosmos.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.670
There is no Halloween in the microcosmos.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:34.430
But if there were, this flatworm would fit
in quite nicely with its spectral appearance.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:39.480
It might be navigating its way through a field
of other organisms, but it looks like it had

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.180
three minutes to prepare for a costume party
and decided to go with the classic “old

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.530
bedsheet with eyeholes” ghost costume.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:55.560
And maybe comparing this flatworm (or even
worms in general) to a ghost is kind of apt.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:02.210
Ghosts straddle two worlds, and so do worms,
though in their case, instead of bridging

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:09.880
the realm of the living and dead, worms manage
to stretch between the micro and the macro.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.380
This creature you’re watching now would
probably be difficult to see without a microscope.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:22.350
But on the other end, there are worms that
are meters too long to observe with a microscope.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:28.370
And akin to ghosts, worms might seem like
a sort of reduced form of life.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:35.280
After all, for a lot of us, worms are the
wiggly tubes that we dug up from playgrounds.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:41.190
And of all of the worms, flatworms are perhaps
the most simple of all.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:47.570
But even digging into what it means to be
the simplest worm makes the whole notion of

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.080
simplicity seem ludicrous.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:02:00.270
So let’s start with the broader question:
what are worms, other than wiggly, self-mating tubes?

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:06.479
While we generally use the word “worm”
to describe many different, unrelated, invertebrate,

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:12.030
tubular animals that belong to a number of
different phyla, there are three main phyla

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.400
that people focus on.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:20.680
The first are our flatworm buddies here, who
are known more formally as Platyhelminthes.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.940
Then there is the phylum Nematoda, known less
formally as roundworms.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:30.340
We’ve come across nematodes many times in
our journey through the microcosmos, which

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:36.959
makes sense given that they are one of the
most abundant animals on this planet.

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:43.180
And last are the Annelida, phylum of our playground
friend, the earthworm.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:49.870
While earthworms are a bit beyond the scope
of the micro, if you will, we’ve also got

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.140
this little Stylaria lacustris.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:03:00.520
At the tip of its head is the proboscis, an
antenna-like structure that the worm uses for feeding.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:09.239
And as we scan past the head, you can see that the length of the Stylaria’s body is segmented.

00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:13.260
Of the three phyla we’ve mentioned, annelids
are the most complex.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:19.959
They have a closed circulatory system with
tubes that transport nutrients and oxygen

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:26.160
throughout their body, as well as a complete
digestive system that has both a mouth to

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.030
take in food and an anus to get rid of waste.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:37.489
If we think of worms in general as a gut enclosed
within a body wall, then that gut is a tube

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:44.790
and the body is a tube, and you can imagine
that worms are a tube inside a tube.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:52.569
Now what separates these different groups is the space that separates one of those tubes from the other.

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:58.840
Complex systems inside annelids are due to
what’s called a coelom, a tissue-lined cavity

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:05.239
that sits between the tube of the digestive
tract and the tube of the body wall.

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:11.459
Importantly, this cavity is filled with fluid,
which facilitates the development of organs

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:15.840
along with the transport of nutrients around
the body.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:22.050
The development of the first coelom was a
very big deal because coeloms don’t just

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:25.250
allow annelids to have complex systems.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:33.580
They connect the seemingly simple organisms we’re talking about here to more complex animals—like us!

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:40.560
Because annelids and humans are both what
is known as coelomates, or eucoelomates.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.540
We are animals that have coeloms.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:50.180
So, thank you to the ancestor that we share
with earthworms who developed the first coelom,

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:55.590
who allowed more complex organisms like you
to exist.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:05:00.810
Nematodes, on the other hand, are what are
called pseudocoelomates.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:06.800
They still have a coelom-like fluid-filled
cavity, but the cavity isn’t lined with

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.020
tissue like you find in true coeloms.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:15.180
Other microscopic pseudocoelomates include
gastrotrichs and rotifers.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:22.699
This pseudocoelom is a slight but significant
difference in nematodes when compared to annelids,

00:05:22.699 --> 00:05:27.260
corresponding to a slightly less complex body plan, though nematodes

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:31.210
still do have a complete digestive system.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.430
The flatworms are the simplest of all.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:38.420
Starting with the coelom: flatworms don’t
actually have one.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.169
They’re considered acoelomates.

00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:46.389
That area between the gut and body wall where
annelids and nematodes have a cavity full

00:05:46.389 --> 00:05:50.460
of fluid is instead full of tissue.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:56.210
This has a few consequences for the flatworm,
but one of the biggest ones is that this area

00:05:56.210 --> 00:06:01.680
can’t support the development of specialized
systems that you see in other animals.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:09.520
The lack of coelom imposes limits on the flatworm’s
body and lifestyle, reducing both its complexity

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.080
and size.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:23.130
But that does not mean the flatworm lives
a simple life.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:30.150
Of the three worm groups, flatworms are the
most like a tube within another tube, except

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.590
that their outer tube is much less cylindrical.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:39.740
The “flat” in “flatworm” is actually
an adaptation: without a circulatory system

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:46.850
or respiratory system, the worm relies on
diffusion across its outer membrane to supply

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:49.500
cells with oxygen.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:56.820
Being flatter gets the cells closer to the
outside and better positioned for diffusion.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:02.050
And without the ability to build a complete
digestive system like those found in nematodes

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:09.110
and annelids, flatworms have to adjust to
life without that most under-appreciated body

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:11.380
part of all: the anus.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:17.850
They take in food through one opening, digest
it in their gut, and then spew the waste back

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.370
out from that same opening.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:27.620
So unlike other organisms who have separate
entrances and exits for their food, the flatworm

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:32.480
must wait to finish digesting before it can
eat again.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:37.270
But this is not the only way flatworms can
get rid of waste.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:44.380
Their body is lined with a special type of
cell called a flame cell that also gets waste

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.380
out of the worm.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:52.590
While it may not have the elegance of an interconnected
set of organs, this system is its own form

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:54.060
of complexity.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:08:04.990
Basically, every flame cell is one single-celled kidney, allowing the worm to just...ooze waste from its skin.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:11.960
And this is just scratching the surface of
the biological intricacy flatworms are capable of.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:18.770
Like many of their more involved worm counterparts,
flatworms can reproduce both asexually and sexually.

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:26.090
And when reproducing sexually, they can mate
with another flatworm, or even with themselves--both

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:29.320
making and fertilizing their own eggs.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:35.749
There’s also those flatworm species that
can regenerate, a trick that our more developed

00:08:35.749 --> 00:08:39.439
bodies are, alas, not capable of.

00:08:39.439 --> 00:09:00.089
Even the many weird ways they move make the
word “wiggle” feel reductive.

00:09:00.089 --> 00:09:06.119
Our perception of simplicity across nature
is built on comparisons.

00:09:06.119 --> 00:09:09.139
Compared to us, an earthworm may seem simple.

00:09:09.139 --> 00:09:15.589
Put that earthworm next to a flatworm, and
suddenly the earthworm represents a gigantic

00:09:15.589 --> 00:09:17.209
biological advancement.

00:09:17.209 --> 00:09:27.550
But then put that flatworm next to a bacteria,
and suddenly it contains multitudes, it is a universe.

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:40.199
That flatworm is a marvel, an almost unbelievable
testament to the power of evolution.

00:09:40.199 --> 00:09:47.209
